# feeds.conf — extra places to send your ADS-B data.
#
# Copy to feeds.conf and uncomment the ones you want:
#     cp feeds.conf.example feeds.conf
#     ./install.sh
#
# Each active line becomes a readsb --net-connector. readsb opens an outbound
# connection per destination, so nothing needs to be exposed to the internet
# and no inbound firewall rules are required.
#
# Format:  <host>  <port>  <protocol>
#
# Protocol is almost always beast_reduce_plus_out — Beast format with reduced
# throughput plus the UUID that identifies your receiver. install.sh generates
# a UUID once and stores it at ~/.config/adsb-rig/uuid.
#
# Endpoints below are from the sdr-enthusiasts ultrafeeder project and were
# verified to accept a connection at the time of writing. Aggregators do move
# hosts occasionally — check the service's own docs if one stops connecting.

# --- Non-profit / community aggregators. No account or key needed. ---

# feed.airplanes.live      30004   beast_reduce_plus_out
# feed.adsb.fi             30004   beast_reduce_plus_out
# in.adsb.lol              30004   beast_reduce_plus_out
# feed.planespotters.net   30004   beast_reduce_plus_out
# feed.theairtraffic.com   30004   beast_reduce_plus_out

# --- ADSBExchange. Community-run, unfiltered. ---

# feed1.adsbexchange.com   30004   beast_reduce_plus_out

# --- Others that appear in the ultrafeeder list ---

# data.avdelphi.com        24999   beast_reduce_plus_out
# dati.flyitalyadsb.com    4905    beast_reduce_plus_out

#
# NOT handled here:
#
#   MLAT (multilateration) needs a separate mlat-client daemon and your exact
#   receiver coordinates. The ports above are ADS-B only.
#
#   FlightAware (piaware), FlightRadar24 (fr24feed), RadarBox and PlaneFinder
#   run their own feeder clients with their own accounts and keys. Point them
#   at this rig's Beast output rather than adding them here — enable
#   --net-bo-port 30005 on readsb and configure their client to read from
#   localhost:30005.
